{"id": "4dd415f0-8aef-49af-bf81-b1801375063a", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T22:04:28.876932", "source": "user", "parent_id": "85be89ce-c3a6-4b9e-8ace-03177b419f03", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"This is a design-only task (per the submission contract): produce a final experiment design with rationale, controls, analysis plan and feasibility; do not run the experiment. Please begin with an initial_inspection turn and provide ONLY the following, in a single structured response: (1) Input/repository inspection: confirm whether the workspace at /workspace contains any relevant RepoBench materials, prior notes, code, or environment specs (Python version, any installed ML libraries such as transformers/torch/datasets, GPU availability). Report what is actually present vs. absent; do not fabricate. (2) Proposed approach: outline how you would compare a small general LM (GPT-2 family, e.g., gpt2 or gpt2-medium) against a larger code-specialized model (CodeLlama-7b, e.g., codellama/CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf or CodeLlama-7b-Instruct) on the RepoBench Python cross_file_first split. Cover: dataset/version, split definition and size, the exact scoring protocol (RepoBench uses edit-similarity / MAELS / EM against the gold continuing line per the original paper), prompt format, context window handling for cross-file inputs, generation hyperparameters (max new tokens, temperature, top-p), and computational feasibility given the environment (no GPU, CPU-only sandbox). (3) Assumptions and uncertainties: list the key assumptions you are making (e.g., model identifiers, dataset access, metric choice, evaluation budget) and the items still needing confirmation from me. Do not begin writing the full design yet; I will review your approach at approach_review before authorizing the main work.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"plan\", \"approach_approved\": false, \"public_task\": {\"question\": \"How does the