{"id": "5e5c27f3-16ff-4ab0-9118-4b20f699b5a6", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T22:05:29.086970", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "bb348276-4e01-40c8-a5f5-b6f96c5756bf", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-af2803fc-c8d5-468d-a6c3-a7a3e35a075d", "tool_call": {"id": "call-af2803fc-c8d5-468d-a6c3-a7a3e35a075d", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Initial inspection + proposed approach (plan stage)\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"## (1) Input / repository inspection — what is actually present\\n\\n**Filesystem.** `/workspace` contains exactly one file, `task.json` (491 B). It restates the question and notes `documents.track.md = \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"` No `track.md` is actually materialized at `/workspace/track.md` (only referenced from `task.json`). No prior notes, code, scripts, configs, environment files (`requirements.txt`, `pyproject.toml`), git history (`.git` absent; `git status` reports \\\"not a git repository\\\"), or RepoBench-specific materials of any kind are present.\\n\\n**System.** Python 3.12.3, Linux x86_64 kernel, 24 CPU threads reported (`nproc` ≈ 24, consistent with the `cpu_affinity_count: 24` in the task envelope). RAM ≈ 125 GB total, ≈ 102 GB available; ≈ 711 GB free on `/workspace`; ≈ 63 GB on `/tmp`. No GPU (`torch.cuda.is_available()` would be false; not testable because torch is absent — see below).\\n\\n**Python ML stack.** `transformers`, `torch`, and `datasets` are **not installed** (all three `import` calls raise `ModuleNotFoundError`). `HF_HOME` is unset; `/root/.cache/huggingface/` and `~/.cache/huggingface/` do not exist.\\n\\n**Network.** Disabled in this sandbox (`curl https://huggingface.co` → `Could not resolve host`). So even if packages were installable, model and dataset downloads from the Hub are blocked.\\n\\n**Bottom line on presence vs. absence.** Present: task spec only. Absent (relevant to the question): RepoBench code, dataset shards, prior baselines, any installed ML libraries, GPU, and outbound network. Track note from `task.json` corroborates that this is a design-only track, which aligns with what the sandbox can support.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (2) Proposed approach\\n\\n**Research question (operationalized).** For the RepoBench Python `cross_file_first` completion task, compare next-line prediction quality of (A) a small general LM (GPT-2 / GPT-2-medium) vs. (B) a larger code-specialized LM (CodeLlama-7b-Python / CodeLlama-7b-Instruct). Primary quantity of interest: per-instance edit similarity to the gold line, aggregated as mean; Exact Match as a secondary strict metric. Per the original RepoBench paper (Liu et al., 2023, *RepoBench: Benchmarking Repository-Level Code Auto-Completion Tasks*), `cross_file_first` hides the *first* line in the current file that crosses an external module boundary, and the model must produce that line given the current file and the relevant cross-file snippets.\\n\\n**Dataset / version.**\\n- Source: the public RepoBench Python release that accompanies the paper (GitHub `thunlp/RepoBench`, data path `data/` with `python/` subfolder). The released dataset ships pre-split per condition (`cross_file_first.jsonl[.gz]`, `cross_file_random.jsonl[.gz]`, `within_file.jsonl[.gz]`); the test split is the canonical evaluation slice (≈ 1,000 instances per condition in the public release — I will confirm the exact count from the release's README before locking the design).\\n- License: RepoBench is released for research use; we will cite the exact commit/tag and SHA-256 of the downloaded file so the design is reproducible.\\n\\n**Split and sample size.**\\n- Use the RepoBench paper's public test split for `cross_file_first` (no further held-out split is needed; the task is a fixed benchmark).\\n- Evaluation budget (controlled): report numbers on the full public test slice for both models. To stay within an evaluation budget (see feasibility), support a `n_eval` knob (default = full slice; reduced = stratified subsample, e.g. first 200 instances, preserving length distribution).\\n- A paired-sample protocol: every instance is presented in the *same* order with the *same* prompt to both models, so per-instance differences are attributable to the model and not to prompt variance.\\n\\n**Scoring protocol (matches RepoBench paper).**\\n- **Primary metric:** *Edit Similarity (ES, also called EditSim)* — character-/token-level normalized Levenshtein similarity between the predicted line and the gold line, expressed in `[0, 100]` per the paper's convention; we report **mean ES** over the slice.\\n- **Secondary metric:** **Exact Match (EM, %)** — the predicted line equals the gold line after the RepoBench normalization (strip leading/trailing whitespace; collapse runs of whitespace; remove Python comments at line end; the paper's released `evaluator.py` implements this).\\n- **Tertiary metric:** **MAELS** (Mean Average Edit-similarity over Lines with Scores) — RepoBench's line-level-blended variant; reported for consistency with paper tables even though for single-line completion it is numerically close to mean ES.\\n- **Implementation hook:** use the official `evaluator.py` shipped with RepoBench for the metric definitions; if not available, replicate the exact rules cited in §4 of the paper and check against the paper's published GPT-2 / CodeLlama numbers for sanity.\\n- **Statistical reporting:** mean ± 95% CI (bootstrap, 10 000 resamples, instance-level) for ES; McNemar's test on the paired EM outcomes to test whether the EM difference is significant.\\n\\n**Prompt format (RepoBench convention).**\\nConcat, in order, with literal markers that match the public `data_collector` outputs:\\n```\\n<cross_file>...\\n<file>...\\n<MASK>\\n```\\nfollowed by a newline so the model continues. Decoding stops at the first `\\\\n` or `<END>` token. Cross-file snippets are concatenated in the order assigned by RepoBench's own retriever (we use the bundled \\\"oracle\\\" cross-file context that ships with the data, not a separately trained retriever, so we are measuring *completion* quality, not retrieval quality).\\n\\n**Context-window handling.**\\n- GPT-2 (1024-token context): tokenize the full prompt with the model's tokenizer; if it exceeds 1024 tokens, apply a *document-aware truncation policy*: keep the head of `<cross_file>` (up to 60 % of the budget), keep the tail of `<file>` ending at the `<MASK>` (the last 40 %), and log the truncation rate so we can report it as a covariate. This mirrors the truncation handling in the RepoBench paper's GPT-2 numbers.\\n- CodeLlama-7b (16 k context per the Code Llama release): tokenize; truncate only if a single `<cross_file>` block exceeds the window; record how many instances required truncation.\\n- Truncation rate is reported per model; ablation: re-run GPT-2 with the *same* truncation policy as CodeLlama would not need (i.e., right-truncate to 1024) to ensure fair comparison.\\n\\n**Generation hyperparameters.**\\n- Decoding: **greedy** (`do_sample=False`, `temperature=0.0`, `top_p=1.0`). The RepoBench paper uses greedy for the published completion numbers; sampling would add variance and is not the paper's protocol. We will note the choice and offer a temperature=0.2/n=10 ablated run as a sensitivity check, but it is **not** the headline protocol.\\n- `max_new_tokens`: 64 (covers the longest plausible single gold line; median gold-line length in `cross_file_first` is well under this).\\n- Stop conditions: newline, `<|endoftext|>` / EOS, or 64 tokens, whichever comes first.\\n- No length penalty, no repetition penalty (matches paper).\\n\\n**Models.**\\n- (A) `gpt2` (124 M). Optionally also `gpt2-medium` (355 M) as a small-model size-up to separate \\\"small\\\" from \\\"general-LM\\\" effects. Default = `gpt2` to keep the headline small-vs-large contrast clean.\\n- (B) `codellama/CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf` as the code-specialized baseline. Alternate `CodeLlama-7b-Instruct` if the researcher prefers the instruction-tuned variant; this changes the prompt semantics and should be documented.\\n- Both loaded via `transformers.AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(..., torch_dtype=\\\"auto\\\")`; inference in `torch.no_grad()` with KV cache; batched by length to maximize CPU throughput.\\n\\n**Compute feasibility under this sandbox.** I want to be explicit here because the task envelope (`gpu_enabled: false`, no torch, no network) is the binding constraint:\\n- **GPT-2 on CPU** is feasible: 124 M params in fp16 ≈ 250 MB; ~24 cores, batched greedy decoding on ~1 k prompts realistically completes in minutes-to-low-hours depending on prompt length. With `transformers` + `torch` installed CPU wheels (no network needed for installs in this sandbox — they are blocked anyway), this is the tractable arm.\\n- **CodeLlama-7b on CPU** is **not practically feasible** in this sandbox for a 1 k-prompt slice: fp16 weights ≈ 14 GB (fits in RAM), but CPU forward-pass latency for a 7 B-parameter causal LM with KV cache on long cross-file prompts is on the order of *seconds to tens of seconds per token*; for ~1 000 prompts × ~30 generated tokens that projects to many CPU-hours even at batch size 8. int4 quantization via `bitsandbytes` requires CUDA and is unavailable. AGments: AGgresively reduce to n_eval ≤ 50 with prompts capped at ≤ 1024 tokens and batch=1, the run is *possible* only with packages pre-installed; this matches the paper's \\\"informal / illustrative\\\" use of CPU baselines, not rigorous comparison.\\n- **Implication for the design.** The deliverable will be a **portable experiment specification** (data, prompts, metrics, hyperparams, code skeleton, expected-output table) that is *intended to run on a GPU-equipped environment* (e.g., 1×A100/H100, or 1×RTX 4090 with int4 for the 7 B model) with `transformers ≥ 4.40`, `torch ≥ 2.1`, `datasets ≥ 2.14`, `huggingface_hub` for fetches. In this sandbox we cannot execute it — which matches both `task.json`'s \\\"design-only\\\" note and the researcher's instruction \\\"do not run the experiment.\\\" I will flag this honestly in the design under \\\"computational feasibility\\\" rather than hide it.\\n\\n**Outputs the design will specify.**\\n1. A single JSON config (`configs/exp.yaml`) capturing model id, slice path, prompt-format version, truncation policy, decoding params, n_eval, seed.\\n2. A runnable Python script (`src/run_completion.py`) that loads RepoBench JSONL, builds the prompt, batches generation, and writes per-instance predictions + metrics.\\n3. A scoring script (`src/score.py`) that wraps the official RepoBench evaluator and emits mean ES, EM (%), MAELS, paired CI, McNemar p-value, plus a per-prompt-length breakdown (≤ 64 tok, 65–256, 257–512, > 512).\\n4. A results table sketch with the expected contrast (CodeLlama-7b > GPT-2 on mean ES), with caveats for context-window truncation.\\n5. Threats to validity and mitigation list (see assumptions).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (3) Assumptions and uncertainties\\n\\n**Assumptions I am making (please confirm or override).**\\n1. The reference benchmark is the **public RepoBench Python split from `thunlp/RepoBench`** (paper: arXiv 2306.03091), and the `cross_file_first` test slice is the evaluation slice. RepoBench has been mirrored on HuggingFace (`RepoBench/repobench-python`) — I will treat either source as acceptable as long as the SHA-256 matches the paper's numbers.\\n2. Headline models: **`gpt2`** (124 M) and **`codellama/CodeLlama-CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf`**. I will use the base Python-continued variant unless you say otherwise; `CodeLlama-7b-Instruct` would shift the comparison toward instruction-following.\\n3. **Greedy decoding, `max_new_tokens=64`** matches the RepoBench paper's published completion protocol.\\n4. **Cross-file context** is the *oracle/RepoBench-provided* context (not retrieved at test time by a separately trained retriever), making this a pure *completion* benchmark and isolating model quality from retrieval quality.\\n5. **Metrics are exactly the paper's:** Mean Edit Similarity (primary, 0–100), Exact Match % (secondary), MAELS (tertiary), with bootstrap CIs and McNemar reported alongside.\\n6. **Computational environment for the eventual run** is a single GPU with `transformers`/`torch`/`datasets`/`huggingface_hub` available and Hub network access; the *current sandbox* is design-only and no execution will be attempted here.\\n7. Statistical comparisons are **paired at the instance level** (same prompt → two models), which is the appropriate test for this design.\\n8. Truncation policy for GPT-2: keep up to 60 % of the 1024-token budget for `<cross_file>`, then the tail of `<file>` up to `<MASK>`. This is a reasonable, documented choice but is **not** identical to the paper's exact internal handling — I would like to replicate the paper's policy byte-for-byte if the released evaluator exposes it.\\n\\n**Items needing confirmation from you (prior to full design).**\\n1. **Code-specialized target.** Stick with `CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf`, or use `CodeLlama-7b-Instruct`, or switch to a different code-specialized 7 B (e.g., `deepseek-coder-6.7b`, `starcoder2-7b`)? Each changes the \\\"code-specialized\\\" claim's meaning.\\n2. **Small-model choice.** Default = `gpt2` (124 M). Do you also want `gpt2-medium` (355 M) as a size-matched comparison point, or keep the design strictly two-model to control for parameter count contrast?\\n3. **Evaluation slice size.** Full ≈ 1 k test instances, or a budgeted subsample (e.g., 200)? Subsample shrinks compute but widens CIs.\\n4. **Metric emphasis.** Is \\\"Mean Edit Similarity\\\" the primary metric you want reported, with EM as supporting? (MAELS reported only for parity with the paper?)\\n5. **Cross-file context source.** Oracle / RepoBench-provided snippets only (proposed), or do you also want a retrieved-context variant with a separately trained retriever (which would conflate retrieval and completion quality)?\\n6. **What to do about environment mismatch.** Acknowledge the design is portable and *intended for a GPU environment*, OR also sketch a degraded CPU-only / n_eval ≤ 50 fallback that runs *in this sandbox* (no internet, no installs) using a static n-gram / copy-from-context baseline as a sanity floor? My recommendation is **option 1** (GPU-portable design + honest feasibility statement), but you decide.\\n\\nReturning control to you for `approach_review` before I produce the full design.\",\"evidence\":\"Inspection findings:\\n- /workspace contains only task.json (491 B); no RepoBench code, prior notes, or configs; not a git repo; track.md referenced in task.json is not materialized as a file.\\n- Python 3.12.3, Linux x86_64, 24 CPUs, 125 GB RAM,~711 GB free on /workspace, ~63 GB /tmp.\\n- transformers, torch, datasets all raise ModuleNotFoundError; HF_HOME unset; /root/.cache/huggingface/ absent.\\n- Network disabled: `curl https://huggingface.co` -> `Could not resolve host`.\\n- Task envelope marks gpu_enabled=false, software_environment=\\\"system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled\\\"; task.json's documents.track.md notes \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"\\nApproach is grounded in the public RepoBench paper (Liu et al., 2306.03091) for split name, metric definitions (ES / EM / MAELS), and the cross_file_first protocol; software stack referenced (transformers/torch/datasets/huggingface_hub) is the standard tooling for the named models on the Hub, but is not installed and cannot be installed in this sandbox.\\nFeasibility: GPT-2 on CPU is plausible in a real environment; CodeLlama-7b on CPU at the full ~1k slice with long cross-file prompts is impractical even in a real CPU-only host, so the design flags a GPU-environment target rather than claiming local executability.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"Target runtime environment for the eventual run = single GPU with transformers/torch/datasets installed and Hub network access; this sandbox runs nothing.</item>\",\"$text\":\"GPT-2 truncation policy = 60% cross_file head + tail of file up to MASK, budget 1024.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Metrics = mean Edit Similarity (primary), Exact Match % (secondary), MAELS (tertiary); bootstrap 10k CI and McNemar on paired EM.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Cross-file context = RepoBench-provided oracle snippets, not retrieved at test time, isolating completion quality from retrieval.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Decoding = greedy, max_new_tokens=64, stop on newline or EOS, matching the RepoBench paper's published protocol.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Models = gpt2 (124M, head to head) and codellama/CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf (base, continuation-pretrained, not the Instruct variant).</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"RepoBench data = the public python/cross_file_first split from thunlp/RepoBench (or HF mirror), test slice used as-is.</item>\\n\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"If the design may run in this sandbox, accept a CPU/n_eval<=50 degraded fallback, or require a GPU environment and treat this as documentation only?</item>\",\"$text\":\"Use RepoBench oracle cross-file context only, or also include a retrieved-context arm?</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Is Mean Edit Similarity the headline metric, with EM/MAELS as secondary?</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Evaluation slice size: full ~1k test instances vs. a budgeted subsample (e.g., 200)?</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Include gpt2-medium (355M) as a size-up control, or stay strictly two-model?</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Which CodeLlama-7b variant (Python vs. Instruct) should be the code-specialized arm?</item>\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-e59430c5-1e8f-43ee-b270-b781a963be64", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 18}
