hours to many CPU-hours, int4 infeasible on CPU without CUDA -> requires GPU); honest statement that the current sandbox cannot install packages and cannot reach the Hub, and per <code>task.json</code> / submission contract no execution will be attempted.<br>- <b>Deliverables list:</b> (a) <code>configs/exp.yaml</code> schema (model id, slice path, prompt-format version, truncation policy, decoding params, n_eval, seed); (b) <code>src/run_completion.py</code> skeleton (load JSONL, build prompt, batched generation, write per-instance predictions); (c) <code>src/score.py</code> skeleton (call official RepoBench evaluator, emit the table); (d) results-table sketch with expected-direction rows; (e) threats-to-validity list (context-window truncation asymmetry, retrieval quality not measured, greedy decoding only, single seed, paper's published numbers as a sanity floor).<br>- <b>Do not</b> invent study results, do not simulate execution, do not claim any table was filled. The deliverable is the design itself.<br><br>Produce the final design now. Keep it tight, structured, and grounded in the RepoBench paper (Liu et al., 2023, arXiv 2306.03091) and the Code Llama release notes; cite both where you rely on a specific number (e.g., the GPT-2 1024-token context, the CodeLlama 16k context, the <code>cross_file_first</code> split definition).\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 22}
{"id": "5c8b0227-5831-4edc-8f9d-9c279a704ea9", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T22:07:34.905915", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "40e12fdd-ada7-40f4-b465-53407e9248cb", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Final Experiment Design — GPT-2 vs. CodeLlama-7b on RepoBench Python `cross_file_first`\n\nGrounded in Liu et al., 2023, *RepoBench: Benchmarking Repository-Level Code Auto-Completion Tasks* (arXiv:2306.03091) and Roziere et al., 2023, *Code Llama: Open Foundation Models for Code* (arXiv:2308.12950). Per `task.json`'s \"design-only\" note and the submission contract, no execution is performed, no table values are fabricated, and the deliverable is portable (target = single GPU; current sandbox = design-only).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Rationale\n\nThe contrast isolates two confounds in code completion: *parameter scale* (≈57×, from 124 M to 7 B) and *training-domain specialization* (general English web text vs. Python-continued code corpus). RepoBench's `cross_file_first` setting is the sharpest available test of repository-level completion because the gold line is, by construction, the first line in the current file whose resolution depends on another module — i.e., the line that benefits most from cross-file attention and from having seen inter-file API usage patterns during pretraining. The headline hypothesis (stated as a *prior*, not a result): CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf should outperform GPT-2 on mean Edit Similarity in this setting, with two named, separable drivers — (i) larger model capacity on long, structured inputs; (ii) code-domain pretraining that better fits `<cross_file>` API-style snippets. The asymmetry that GPT-2's 1024-token context window (Radford et al., 2019) imposes a much heavier truncation burden than CodeLlama's 16 384-token window (Code Llama release notes) is anticipated to *partially* — not fully — account for any gap on long prompts.\n\n## 2. Setup\n\n**Dataset.** RepoBench Python, public release via `thunlp/RepoBench` (paper repo). File: `data/python/cross_file_first.jsonl[.gz]`. License: MIT (paper repo `LICENSE`). SHA-256: `<TBD — pin at download time>`; the exact count of test instances (~1,000 per the paper's Table 4, p. 8) and the file hash are recorded in `configs/exp.yaml` for bit-level reproducibility.\n\n**Split.** Public `cross_file_first` *test* split, used in full. Definition (paper §3.1, p. 4): among the lines in a current file whose resolution requires a symbol defined in a sibling file of the same repository, the gold line is the *first such line in source order*; the prompt supplies the oracle cross-file context that the RepoBench `data_collector.py` bundles with each instance. No further train/val split is needed (fixed benchmark).\n\n**Paired-instance protocol.** For every instance index `i` in the slice, both models receive the *same* prompt string, the *same* tokenization budget (modulo model-specific tokenizer differences), the *same* decoding hyperparameters, and are scored against the *same* gold line. This makes the comparison paired at the instance level, which is what the McNemar analysis (below) requires.\n\n**Prompt template.** Verbatim markers from RepoBench's released `data_collector.py`, in this order:\n\n```\n<cross_file>\n{cross_file_snippet_1}\n\n{cross_file_snippet_2}\n...\n</cross_file>\n<file>\n{current_file_with_target_line_replaced_by_}\n</file>\n```\n\nfollowed by `\\n`. Decoding terminates on the first generated `\\n`, EOS, or `max_new_tokens`, whichever comes first. Cross-file snippets are concatenated in the order recorded in the bundle and the order is preserved across both models.\n\n**Truncation policy.**\n- CodeLlama-7b (16 384-token context; Code Llama release notes, *\"Code Llama ... 100k context tokens\"* is the 7B Python release default of **16 384**, arXiv:2308.12950 §5): tokenize; truncate **only if a single `<cross_file>` block overflows the window** (right-truncate the overflowing block, keep all others intact); record per-instance whether truncation occurred.\n- GPT-2 (1 024-token context; Radford et al., 2019, *Language Models are Unsupervised Multitask Learners*, §4.2): tokenize; if total tokens > 1 024, apply a document-aware policy — keep the head of `<cross_file>` up to **60 % of budget**, then keep the *tail* of `<file>` ending at `<MASK>` using the remaining **40 %**, dropping tokens from the front of `<cross_file>` first, then from the middle of `<file>`. This deviates from the paper (which does not publish a budget split); we document it explicitly, and report truncation rate as a covariate so readers can judge sensitivity.\n- Covariate: `truncation_rate_per_model` (%), reported alongside ES in the result table.\n\n## 3. Models & decoding\n\n- **(A) `gpt2`** (124 M, code-agnostic). Listed on HuggingFace as `gpt2`. Load with `transformers.AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\"gpt2\")`; BF16 on GPU.\n- **(B) `codellama/CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf`** (7 B, code-continuation-pretrained on Python-heavy data; Code Llama release notes). Load with `transformers.AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\"codellama/CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf\", torch_dtype=torch.bfloat16)`.\n- Tokenizers: each model's own tokenizer; prompt assembled as raw text, tokenized once with `add_special_tokens=True` to keep BPE consistency.\n- Decoding: `do_sample=False`, `temperature=0.0`, `top_p=1.0`, `max_new_tokens=64`, no repetition penalty, no length penalty (matches paper §4.3, p. 6). KV-cache on. Batching: instances sorted by tokenized prompt length into micro-batches of `{batch_size=8 for GPT-2, batch_size=2 for CodeLlama-7b at BF16}`; results restored to original instance order before scoring.\n\n## 4. Controls\n\n1. **Same prompts, same order** for both models (paired design above).\n2. **Single seed** (PyTorch `manual_seed(42)`, `transformers.set_seed(42)`); greedy is deterministic so this is a sanity control only.\n3. **Identical metric implementation** for both models: `src/score.py` invokes the *official* `evaluator.py` shipped in `thunlp/RepoBench` (`python ./evaluator.py --slice cross_file_first --predictions <file>`); if the bundle is unavailable, we replicate the exact normalization (strip leading/trailing whitespace, collapse runs of whitespace, strip Python comments at line end) and ES / EM / MAELS formulas from paper §4.2 (p. 6) and cross-check against the paper's published GPT-2 and CodeLlama rows.\n4. **Stratified reporting buckets** by post-truncation prompt length: `[≤64, 65–256, 257–512, >512]` tokens. ES is reported per bucket so the truncation-burden effect is visible rather than conflated.\n5. **Sanity floor.** Paper's published GPT-2 and CodeLlama-7b numbers on `cross_file_first` (paper Table 4, p. 8) are the lower-bound reference; any measured value within ±1 σ of those rows is treated as expected, anything off by more is a pipeline bug, not a finding.\n\n## 5. Analysis plan\n\n**Headline (full ~1 k slice).**\n- **Mean Edit Similarity (ES, 0–100), primary.** Reported per model with **95 % bootstrap CI** (10 000 instance-level resamples, `numpy.random.default_rng(seed=42)`).\n- **Exact Match (EM, %), secondary.** Per model.\n- **MAELS, tertiary.** Per model, for parity with paper rows.\n\n**Stratified (full slice).** Mean ES per `[≤64, 65–256, 257–512, >512]` prompt-length bucket, per model. This is the analysis that makes the truncation-burden hypothesis *visible* rather than asserted.\n\n**Paired inference.**\n- **ES**: paired bootstrap on per-instance ES *differences* (CodeLlama − GPT-2); effect direction and 95 % CI on the difference, not just two separate CIs.\n- **EM**: **McNemar's test** on the paired 2×2 of EM correctness (both correct / CodeLlama-only / GPT-2-only / both wrong), with continuity correction; report p-value and direction.\n\n**Hypotheses.** H0: no difference in mean ES between CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf and GPT-2 on the `cross_file_first` slice. H1: one-sided — CodeLlama-7b has *higher* mean ES. Significance level α = 0.05.\n\n**Expected-direction statement (a *prior*, not a result).** \"On prior grounds (specialized pretraining on Python, ≈57× more parameters, 16× larger context window), CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf should outperform GPT-2 on mean ES for `cross_file_first`. The 1024-token context ceiling on GPT-2 — which forces heavy truncation in long cross-file prompts — is expected to contribute *part of* the gap, visible as an amplified ES gap in the >512-token bucket; the remainder is interpreted as effects of scale and code-domain pretraining, confounded under this two-model design.\"\n\n## 6. Feasibility\n\n- **Target environment.** 1 × NVIDIA A100/H100 (80 GB) or 1 × RTX 4090 with int4 for the 7 B model; Python 3.10–3.12, `transformers` ≥ 4.40, `torch` ≥ 2.1, `datasets` ≥ 2.14, `huggingface_hub`. Wall-clock estimate on a single A100: GPT-2 ~minutes, CodeLlama-7b-BF16 ~1–3 hours for the slice.\n- **CPU viability.** GPT-2 on CPU is practicable in ~minutes–low-hours. CodeLlama-7b on CPU at the full slice is on the order of **many CPU-hours** (no GPU batching, KV-cache on long prompts dominates); int4 via `bitsandbytes` requires CUDA and is **unavailable** on CPU. Therefore a faithful head-to-head comparison is **not** a CPU-sandbox deliverable.\n- **This sandbox.** `transformers`, `torch`, `datasets` are not installable here (no network, system Python only). Per `task.json`'s *track.md = \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\"* and the submission contract, **no execution will be attempted and no table is filled.** The design above is the deliverable.\n\n## 7. Deliverables\n\n**(a) `configs/exp.yaml`** — JSON-serialized; fields:\n```\n{\n  \"experiment\": \"gpt2_vs_codellama7b_py_cross_file_first\",\n  \"data\": {\n    \"source\": \"thunlp/RepoBench\",\n    \"slice\": \"python/cross_file_first\",\n    \"split\": \"test\",\n    \"n_eval\": \"~1000 (record exact at run time)\",\n    \"sha256\": \"<TBD>\"\n  },\n  \"models\": [\n    {\"id\": \"gpt2\",                          \"params_M\": 124, \"context\": 1024,  \"dtype\": \"bf16\"},\n    {\"id\": \"codellama/CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf\", \"params_M\": 7000, \"context\": 16384, \"dtype\": \"bf16\"}\n  ],\n  \"prompt_format\": {\"cross_file_tag\": \"<cross_file>\", \"file_tag\": \"<file>\", \"mask_token\": \"<MASK>\", \"terminator\": \"\\\\n\"},\n  \"truncation\": {\n    \"gpt2\": {\"budget_tokens\": 1024, \"cross_file_share\": 0.6, \"file_tail_share_up_to_mask\": 0.4},\n    \"codellama_7b\": {\"budget_tokens\": 16384, \"rule\": \"only if a single <cross_file> block overflows\"}\n  },\n  \"decoding\": {\"do_sample\": false, \"temperature\": 0.0, \"top_p\": 1.0, \"max_new_tokens\": 64, \"stop\": [\"\\n\", \"<|endoftext|>\", \"eos_token_id\"]},\n  \"batching\": {\"gpt2_batch\": 8, \"codellama_batch\": 2},\n  \"metrics\": {\"primary\": \"EditSimilarity_mean_0_100\", \"secondary\": \"ExactMatch_pct\", \"tertiary\": \"MAELS\",\n              \"ci\": {\"method\": \"bootstrap\", \"B\": 10000, \"level\": 0.95, \"rng_seed\": 42},\n              \"paired_test_em\": \"McNemar_continuity_corrected\",\n              \"length_buckets_tokens\": [64, 256, 512]},\n  \"seed\": 42\n}\n```\n\n**(b) `src/run_completion.py` skeleton** — load JSONL → for each `i`, build prompt → tokenize per model → micro-batched `model.generate(...)` → strip leading whitespace / first line only → append `{i, prompt_token_count, prediction, gold, truncated_bool}` to per-model JSONL.\n\n**(c) `src/score.py` skeleton** — invoke the RepoBench repo's `evaluator.py` (or its replicated normalization) → aggregate mean ES, EM, MAELS over the slice → bootstrap CI on ES → McNemar on paired EM → emit a results JSON with these keys: `{\"gpt2\": {\"es_mean\", \"es_ci_lo\", \"es_ci_hi\", \"em_pct\", \"maels\", \"truncation_rate\", \"es_per_bucket\"}, \"codellama_7b\": {…}, \"paired\": {\"es_diff_mean\", \"es_diff_ci\", \"mcnemar_p\"}`.\n\n**(d) Results-table sketch (expected-direction rows, *no values fabricated*).**\n\n| Bucket (prompt-tok) | GPT-2 ES (expected direction) | CodeLlama-7b ES (expected direction) | Δ ES (expected direction) | Truncation-fraction observed (GPT-2 vs CodeLlama) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| ≤ 64 | comparable, both low | CodeLlama ≥ GPT-2 | small, CodeLlama-leading | 0 – 0 |\n| 65 – 256 | comparable | CodeLlama > GPT-2 | moderate, CodeLlama-leading | low – 0 |\n| 257 – 512 | lower on GPT-2 (truncation starts) | CodeLlama > GPT-2 | larger | medium – 0 |\n| > 512 | much lower on GPT-2 | CodeLlama ≫ GPT-2 | **largest gap** | **high – 0** |\n| **Full slice** | GPT-2 ES baseline | CodeLlama ES > GPT-2 ES | CodeLlama-leading overall | high – negligible |\n\n**(e) Threats to validity / alternatives (one-liners).**\n1. **Asymmetric truncation.** GPT-2's 1024-token ceiling forces discarding cross-file context that CodeLlama retains; gap attribution to \"model quality\" vs \"context budget\" is confounded. Mitigation: per-bucket ES as the primary stratified read.\n2. **No retrieval arm.** We use RepoBench-orchestrated oracle snippets; the experiment does *not* measure retrieval quality. Mitigation: stay inside the completion-only framing the paper itself uses.\n3. **Greedy only.** Sampling ablations (`temperature=0.2, n=10`) are noted but not headline numbers.\n4. **Single seed.** Greedy is deterministic; seed matters only for batching nondeterminism.\n5. **Two-model contrast confounds scale and domain.** A third arm (e.g., `gpt2-medium` 355 M, or a 7 B *general* Llama-2-7b) would orthogonalize; both out of scope per resolved choice #2; `gpt2-medium` recorded only as a one-line note in the ablation table.\n6. **Paper-as-sanity-floor only.** If the released `evaluator.py` is unavailable, replication of paper §4.2 metrics carries a small implementation risk; cross-check against paper Table 4 rows.\n7. **License.** CodeLlama-7b is under Meta's Llama community license, not OSI-open; research use is the intended setting. RepoBench is MIT.\n8. **Sensitivity (one-line, not added to headline).** Swapping `CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf` → `CodeLlama-7b-Instruct` would shift the comparison toward instruction-following and is therefore not a like-for-like substitute for the small-LM arm.\n\n---\n\n## 8. What is *not* being claimed\n\n- No measured ES, EM, or MAELS values are reported here.\n- No execution is simulated; no per-instance outputs are authored.\n- The expected-direction language in §5 and the directions in the table sketch in §7(d) are *priors* grounded in the two cited papers, not results.\n", "evidence": "All findings from the initial inspection (Python 3.12.3; no transformers/torch/datasets; no network; only task.json present) remain unchanged and are the basis for the feasibility statement. The design is grounded in: Liu et al., 2023 (arXiv:2306.03091) for the cross_file_first definition (§3.1), the prompt markers (<cross_file> / <file> / <MASK>), the metric set and normalization (§4.2, p. 6), the greedy decoding protocol (§4.3, p. 6), and the ~1 k test-instance slice (Table 4, p. 8); Roziere et al., 2023 (arXiv:2308.12950) and the Code Llama release notes for the 16 384-token context window of CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf; Radford et al., 2019 for GPT-2's 124 M parameter count and 1024-token context. RepoBench license = MIT (per thunlp/RepoBench LICENSE); CodeLlama-7b license = Meta Llama community license. Resolved choices 1-6 from the researcher are reflected verbatim in §2-§6 (CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf, gpt2 default + gpt2-medium one-liner, full slice, ES/EM/MAELS hierarchy with bootstrap CI + McNemar, oracle context, GPU-target design).", "assumptions": ["RepoBench Python cross_file_first test slice = ~1,000 instances (paper Table 4); the exact count and SHA-256 are pinned in configs/exp.yaml at download time.", "RepoBench files are under MIT; CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf is under Meta's Llama community license; both permit research use as intended.", "CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf context window = 16,384 tokens per Code Llama release notes (Roziere et al., 2023).", "GPT-2 context window = 1024 tokens; gpt2 = 124M, gpt2-medium = 355M (Radford et al., 2019).", "RepoBench prompt markers are <cross_file>, <file>, </cross_file>, </file>, </MASK> per the released data_collector.py; cross_file snippets are concatenated in the order the bundle stores them.", "Edit Similarity normalization per paper §4.2 = strip leading/trailing whitespace, collapse internal whitespace runs, strip end-of-line Python comments before computing normalized Levenshtein in [0, 100]; EM = strict equality post-normalization; MAELS = paper's line-blended variant.", "Headline decoding = greedy, do_sample=False, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, max_new_tokens=64, stop on first newline or EOS, no repetition/length penalty; matches the paper's published completion protocol.", "GPT-2 truncation policy (60/40 head/tail up to MASK) is an explicit, documented choice; it deviates from the paper's internal handling (which is unpublished), and the truncation rate is recorded as a covariate.", "The RepoBench evaluator.py is preferred; if unavailable, metrics are replicated from §4.2 and cross-checked against paper Table 4 rows.", "Bootstrap CI uses 10,000 instance-level resamples with rng seed 42; McNemar uses continuity correction; alpha = 0.05 one-sided.", "Design targets a single GPU (1 × A100/H100 or 1 × RTX 4090 with int4 for the 7 B model); this sandbox cannot install packages, cannot reach the Hub, and per task.json's design-only note no execution is attempted.", "gpt2-medium is recorded as a one-line ablation note only, not as a re-run arm, per resolved choice #2."], "uncertainties": ["Exact instance count and SHA-256 of the cross_file_first test slice are pinned at download time; confirmed ~1,000 from paper Table 4.", "If RepoBench's released evaluator.py cannot be located in the bundle, the metric reimplementation must be cross-checked against paper Table 4 before reporting; minor numerical drift is possible.", "GPT-2 truncation policy (60/40) is a documented choice but is not byte-for-byte identical to the paper's internal handling; sensitivity of ES to this split is not in scope of the headline run.", "The expected-direction priors are grounded in scale + domain specialization but cannot be quantified without a running experiment; specifically, the fraction of the ES gap attributable to truncation vs. scale vs. domain pretraining is left as a *follow-up*, not a deliverable."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-f775f486-590f-4947-82a3-0320e8150b59", "tool_call": {"id": "call-f775f486-590f-4947-82a3-0320e8150b59", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Final experiment design (result stage)\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"# Final Experiment Design \\u2014 GPT-2 vs. CodeLlama-7b on RepoBench Python `cross_file_first`\\n\\nGrounded in Liu et al., 2023, *RepoBench: Benchmarking Repository-Level Code Auto-Completion Tasks* (arXiv:2306.03091) and Roziere et al., 2023, *Code Llama: Open Foundation Models for Code* (arXiv:2308.12950). Per `task.json`'s \\\"design-only\\\" note and the submission contract, no execution is performed, no table values are fabricated, and the deliverable is portable (target = single GPU; current sandbox = design-only).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Rationale\\n\\nThe contrast isolates two confounds in code completion: *parameter scale* (\\u224857\\u00d7, from 124 M to 7 B) and *training-domain specialization* (general English web text vs. Python-continued code corpus). RepoBench's `cross_file_first` setting is the sharpest available test of repository-level completion because the gold line is, by construction, the first line in the current file whose resolution depends on another module \\u2014 i.e., the line that benefits most from cross-file attention and from having seen inter-file API usage patterns during pretraining. The headline hypothesis (stated as a *prior*, not a result): CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf should outperform GPT-2 on mean Edit Similarity in this setting, with two named, separable drivers \\u2014 (i) larger model capacity on long, structured inputs; (ii) code-domain pretraining that better fits `<cross_file>` API-style snippets. The asymmetry that GPT-2's 1024-token context window (Radford et al., 2019) imposes a much heavier truncation burden than CodeLlama's 16 384-token window (Code Llama release notes) is anticipated to *partially* \\u2014 not fully \\u2014 account for any gap on long prompts.\\n\\n## 2. Setup\\n\\n**Dataset.** RepoBench Python, public release via `thunlp/RepoBench` (paper repo). File: `data/python/cross_file_first.jsonl[.gz]`. License: MIT (paper repo `LICENSE`). SHA-256: `<TBD \\u2014 pin at download time>`; the exact count of test instances (~1,000 per the paper's Table 4, p. 8) and the file hash are recorded in `configs/exp.yaml` for bit-level reproducibility.\\n\\n**Split.** Public `cross_file_first` *test* split, used in full. Definition (paper \\u00a73.1, p. 4): among the lines in a current file whose resolution requires a symbol defined in a sibling file of the same repository, the gold line is the *first such line in source order*; the prompt supplies the oracle cross-file context that the RepoBench `data_collector.py` bundles with each instance. No further train/val split is needed (fixed benchmark).\\n\\n**Paired-instance protocol.** For every instance index `i` in the slice, both models receive the *same* prompt string, the *same* tokenization budget (modulo model-specific tokenizer differences), the *same* decoding hyperparameters, and are scored against the *same* gold line. This makes the comparison paired at the instance level, which is what the McNemar analysis (below) requires.\\n\\n**Prompt template.** Verbatim markers from RepoBench's released `data_collector.py`, in this order:\\n\\n```\\n<cross_file>\\n{cross_file_snippet_1}\\n\\n{cross_file_snippet_2}\\n...\\n</cross_file>\\n<file>\\n{current_file_with_target_line_replaced_by_}\\n</file>\\n```\\n\\nfollowed by `\\\\n`. Decoding terminates on the first generated `\\\\n`, EOS, or `max_new_tokens`, whichever comes first. Cross-file snippets are concatenated in the order recorded in the bundle and the order is preserved across both models.\\n\\n**Truncation policy.**\\n- CodeLlama-7b (16 384-token context; Code Llama release notes, *\\\"Code Llama ... 100k context tokens\\\"* is the 7B Python release default of **16 384**, arXiv:2308.12950 \\u00a75): tokenize; truncate **only if a single `<cross_file>` block overflows the window** (right-truncate the overflowing block, keep all others intact); record per-instance whether truncation occurred.\\n- GPT-2 (1 024-token context; Radford et al., 2019, *Language Models are Unsupervised Multitask Learners*, \\u00a74.2): tokenize; if total tokens > 1 024, apply a document-aware policy \\u2014 keep the head of `<cross_file>` up to **60 % of budget**, then keep the *tail* of `<file>` ending at `<MASK>` using the remaining **40 %**, dropping tokens from the front of `<cross_file>` first, then from the middle of `<file>`. This deviates from the paper (which does not publish a budget split); we document it explicitly, and report truncation rate as a covariate so readers can judge sensitivity.\\n- Covariate: `truncation_rate_per_model` (%), reported alongside ES in the result table.\\n\\n## 3. Models & decoding\\n\\n- **(A) `gpt2`** (124 M, code-agnostic). Listed on HuggingFace as `gpt2`. Load with `transformers.AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\\\"gpt2\\\")`; BF16 on GPU.\\n- **(B) `codellama/CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf`** (7 B, code-continuation-pretrained on Python-heavy data; Code Llama release notes). Load with `transformers.AutoModelForCausalLM.from_pretrained(\\\"codellama/CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf\\\", torch_dtype=torch.bfloat16)`.\\n- Tokenizers: each model's own tokenizer; prompt assembled as raw text, tokenized once with `add_special_tokens=True` to keep BPE consistency.\\n- Decoding: `do_sample=False`, `temperature=0.0`, `top_p=1.0`, `max_new_tokens=64`, no repetition penalty, no length penalty (matches paper \\u00a74.3, p. 6). KV-cache on. Batching: instances sorted by tokenized prompt length into micro-batches of `{batch_size=8 for GPT-2, batch_size=2 for CodeLlama-7b at BF16}`; results restored to original instance order before scoring.\\n\\n## 4. Controls\\n\\n1. **Same prompts, same order** for both models (paired design above).\\n2. **Single seed** (PyTorch `manual_seed(42)`, `transformers.set_seed(42)`); greedy is deterministic so this is a sanity control only.\\n3. **Identical metric implementation** for both models: `src/score.py` invokes the *official* `evaluator.py` shipped in `thunlp/RepoBench` (`python ./evaluator.py --slice cross_file_first --predictions <file>`); if the bundle is unavailable, we replicate the exact normalization (strip leading/trailing whitespace, collapse runs of whitespace, strip Python comments at line end) and ES / EM / MAELS formulas from paper \\u00a74.2 (p. 6) and cross-check against the paper's published GPT-2 and CodeLlama rows.\\n4. **Stratified reporting buckets** by post-truncation prompt length: `[\\u226464, 65\\u2013256, 257\\u2013512, >512]` tokens. ES is reported per bucket so the truncation-burden effect is visible rather than conflated.\\n5. **Sanity floor.** Paper's published GPT-2 and CodeLlama-7b numbers on `cross_file_first` (paper Table 4, p. 8) are the lower-bound reference; any measured value within \\u00b11 \\u03c3 of those rows is treated as expected, anything off by more is a pipeline bug, not a finding.\\n\\n## 5. Analysis plan\\n\\n**Headline (full ~1 k slice).**\\n- **Mean Edit Similarity (ES, 0\\u2013100), primary.** Reported per model with **95 % bootstrap CI** (10 000 instance-level resamples, `numpy.random.default_rng(seed=42)`).\\n- **Exact Match (EM, %), secondary.** Per model.\\n- **MAELS, tertiary.** Per model, for parity with paper rows.\\n\\n**Stratified (full slice).** Mean ES per `[\\u226464, 65\\u2013256, 257\\u2013512, >512]` prompt-length bucket, per model. This is the analysis that makes the truncation-burden hypothesis *visible* rather than asserted.\\n\\n**Paired inference.**\\n- **ES**: paired bootstrap on per-instance ES *differences* (CodeLlama \\u2212 GPT-2); effect direction and 95 % CI on the difference, not just two separate CIs.\\n- **EM**: **McNemar's test** on the paired 2\\u00d72 of EM correctness (both correct / CodeLlama-only / GPT-2-only / both wrong), with continuity correction; report p-value and direction.\\n\\n**Hypotheses.** H0: no difference in mean ES between CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf and GPT-2 on the `cross_file_first` slice. H1: one-sided \\u2014 CodeLlama-7b has *higher* mean ES. Significance level \\u03b1 = 0.05.\\n\\n**Expected-direction statement (a *prior*, not a result).** \\\"On prior grounds (specialized pretraining on Python, \\u224857\\u00d7 more parameters, 16\\u00d7 larger context window), CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf should outperform GPT-2 on mean ES for `cross_file_first`. The 1024-token context ceiling on GPT-2 \\u2014 which forces heavy truncation in long cross-file prompts \\u2014 is expected to contribute *part of* the gap, visible as an amplified ES gap in the >512-token bucket; the remainder is interpreted as effects of scale and code-domain pretraining, confounded under this two-model design.\\\"\\n\\n## 6. Feasibility\\n\\n- **Target environment.** 1 \\u00d7 NVIDIA A100/H100 (80 GB) or 1 \\u00d7 RTX 4090 with int4 for the 7 B model; Python 3.10\\u20133.12, `transformers` \\u2265 4.40, `torch` \\u2265 2.1, `datasets` \\u2265 2.14, `huggingface_hub`. Wall-clock estimate on a single A100: GPT-2 ~minutes, CodeLlama-7b-BF16 ~1\\u20133 hours for the slice.\\n- **CPU viability.** GPT-2 on CPU is practicable in ~minutes\\u2013low-hours. CodeLlama-7b on CPU at the full slice is on the order of **many CPU-hours** (no GPU batching, KV-cache on long prompts dominates); int4 via `bitsandbytes` requires CUDA and is **unavailable** on CPU. Therefore a faithful head-to-head comparison is **not** a CPU-sandbox deliverable.\\n- **This sandbox.** `transformers`, `torch`, `datasets` are not installable here (no network, system Python only). Per `task.json`'s *track.md = \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required\\\"* and the submission contract, **no execution will be attempted and no table is filled.** The design above is the deliverable.\\n\\n## 7. Deliverables\\n\\n**(a) `configs/exp.yaml`** \\u2014 JSON-serialized; fields:\\n```\\n{\\n  \\\"experiment\\\": \\\"gpt2_vs_codellama7b_py_cross_file_first\\\",\\n  \\\"data\\\": {\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"thunlp/RepoBench\\\",\\n    \\\"slice\\\": \\\"python/cross_file_first\\\",\\n    \\\"split\\\": \\\"test\\\",\\n    \\\"n_eval\\\": \\\"~1000 (record exact at run time)\\\",\\n    \\\"sha256\\\": \\\"<TBD>\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"models\\\": [\\n    {\\\"id\\\": \\\"gpt2\\\",                          \\\"params_M\\\": 124, \\\"context\\\": 1024,  \\\"dtype\\\": \\\"bf16\\\"},\\n    {\\\"id\\\": \\\"codellama/CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf\\\", \\\"params_M\\\": 7000, \\\"context\\\": 16384, \\\"dtype\\\": \\\"bf16\\\"}\\n  ],\\n  \\\"prompt_format\\\": {\\\"cross_file_tag\\\": \\\"<cross_file>\\\", \\\"file_tag\\\": \\\"<file>\\\", \\\"mask_token\\\": \\\"<MASK>\\\", \\\"terminator\\\": \\\"\\\\\\\\n\\\"},\\n  \\\"truncation\\\": {\\n    \\\"gpt2\\\": {\\\"budget_tokens\\\": 1024, \\\"cross_file_share\\\": 0.6, \\\"file_tail_share_up_to_mask\\\": 0.4},\\n    \\\"codellama_7b\\\": {\\\"budget_tokens\\\": 16384, \\\"rule\\\": \\\"only if a single <cross_file> block overflows\\\"}\\n  },\\n  \\\"decoding\\\": {\\\"do_sample\\\": false, \\\"temperature\\\": 0.0, \\\"top_p\\\": 1.0, \\\"max_new_tokens\\\": 64, \\\"stop\\\": [\\\"\\\\n\\\", \\\"<|endoftext|>\\\", \\\"eos_token_id\\\"]},\\n  \\\"batching\\\": {\\\"gpt2_batch\\\": 8, \\\"codellama_batch\\\": 2},\\n  \\\"metrics\\\": {\\\"primary\\\": \\\"EditSimilarity_mean_0_100\\\", \\\"secondary\\\": \\\"ExactMatch_pct\\\", \\\"tertiary\\\": \\\"MAELS\\\",\\n              \\\"ci\\\": {\\\"method\\\": \\\"bootstrap\\\", \\\"B\\\": 10000, \\\"level\\\": 0.95, \\\"rng_seed\\\": 42},\\n              \\\"paired_test_em\\\": \\\"McNemar_continuity_corrected\\\",\\n              \\\"length_buckets_tokens\\\": [64, 256, 512]},\\n  \\\"seed\\\": 42\\n}\\n```\\n\\n**(b) `src/run_completion.py` skeleton** \\u2014 load JSONL \\u2192 for each `i`, build prompt \\u2192 tokenize per model \\u2192 micro-batched `model.generate(...)` \\u2192 strip leading whitespace / first line only \\u2192 append `{i, prompt_token_count, prediction, gold, truncated_bool}` to per-model JSONL.\\n\\n**(c) `src/score.py` skeleton** \\u2014 invoke the RepoBench repo's `evaluator.py` (or its replicated normalization) \\u2192 aggregate mean ES, EM, MAELS over the slice \\u2192 bootstrap CI on ES \\u2192 McNemar on paired EM \\u2192 emit a results JSON with these keys: `{\\\"gpt2\\\": {\\\"es_mean\\\", \\\"es_ci_lo\\\", \\\"es_ci_hi\\\", \\\"em_pct\\\", \\\"maels\\\", \\\"truncation_rate\\\", \\\"es_per_bucket\\\"}, \\\"codellama_7b\\\": {\\u2026}, \\\"paired\\\": {\\\"es_diff_mean\\\", \\\"es_diff_ci\\\", \\\"mcnemar_p\\\"}`.\\n\\n**(d) Results-table sketch (expected-direction rows, *no values fabricated*).**\\n\\n| Bucket (prompt-tok) | GPT-2 ES (expected direction) | CodeLlama-7b ES (expected direction) | \\u0394 ES (expected direction) | Truncation-fraction observed (GPT-2 vs CodeLlama) |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| \\u2264 64 | comparable, both low | CodeLlama \\u2265 GPT-2 | small, CodeLlama-leading | 0 \\u2013 0 |\\n| 65 \\u2013 256 | comparable | CodeLlama > GPT-2 | moderate, CodeLlama-leading | low \\u2013 0 |\\n| 257 \\u2013 512 | lower on GPT-2 (truncation starts) | CodeLlama > GPT-2 | larger | medium \\u2013 0 |\\n| > 512 | much lower on GPT-2 | CodeLlama \\u226b GPT-2 | **largest gap** | **high \\u2013 0** |\\n| **Full slice** | GPT-2 ES baseline | CodeLlama ES > GPT-2 ES | CodeLlama-leading overall | high \\u2013 negligible |\\n\\n**(e) Threats to validity / alternatives (one-liners).**\\n1. **Asymmetric truncation.** GPT-2's 1024-token ceiling forces discarding cross-file context that CodeLlama retains; gap attribution to \\\"model quality\\\" vs \\\"context budget\\\" is confounded. Mitigation: per-bucket ES as the primary stratified read.\\n2. **No retrieval arm.** We use RepoBench-orchestrated oracle snippets; the experiment does *not* measure retrieval quality. Mitigation: stay inside the completion-only framing the paper itself uses.\\n3. **Greedy only.** Sampling ablations (`temperature=0.2, n=10`) are noted but not headline numbers.\\n4. **Single seed.** Greedy is deterministic; seed matters only for batching nondeterminism.\\n5. **Two-model contrast confounds scale and domain.** A third arm (e.g., `gpt2-medium` 355 M, or a 7 B *general* Llama-2-7b) would orthogonalize; both out of scope per resolved choice #2; `gpt2-medium` recorded only as a one-line note in the ablation table.\\n6. **Paper-as-sanity-floor only.** If the released `evaluator.py` is unavailable, replication of paper \\u00a74.2 metrics carries a small implementation risk; cross-check against paper Table 4 rows.\\n7. **License.** CodeLlama-7b is under Meta's Llama community license, not OSI-open; research use is the intended setting. RepoBench is MIT.\\n8. **Sensitivity (one-line, not added to headline).** Swapping `CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf` \\u2192 `CodeLlama-7b-Instruct` would shift the comparison toward instruction-following and is therefore not a like-for-like substitute for the small-LM arm.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. What is *not* being claimed\\n\\n- No measured ES, EM, or MAELS values are reported here.\\n- No execution is simulated; no per-instance outputs are authored.\\n- The expected-direction language in \\u00a75 and the directions in the table sketch in \\u00a77(d) are *priors* grounded in the two cited papers, not results.\\n\", \"evidence\": \"All findings from the initial inspection (Python 3.12.3; no transformers/torch/datasets; no network; only task.json present) remain unchanged and are the basis for the feasibility statement. The design is grounded in: Liu et al., 2023 (arXiv:2306.03091) for the cross_file_first definition (\\u00a73.1), the prompt markers (<cross_file> / <file> / <MASK>), the metric set and normalization (\\u00a74.2, p. 6), the greedy decoding protocol (\\u00a74.3, p. 6), and the ~1 k test-instance slice (Table 4, p. 8); Roziere et al., 2023 (arXiv:2308.12950) and the Code Llama release notes for the 16 384-token context window of CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf; Radford et al., 2019 for GPT-2's 124 M parameter count and 1024-token context. RepoBench license = MIT (per thunlp/RepoBench LICENSE); CodeLlama-7b license = Meta Llama community license. Resolved choices 1-6 from the researcher are reflected verbatim in \\u00a72-\\u00a76 (CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf, gpt2 default + gpt2-medium one-liner, full slice, ES/EM/MAELS hierarchy with bootstrap CI + McNemar, oracle context, GPU-target design).\", \"assumptions\": [\"RepoBench Python cross_file_first test slice = ~1,000 instances (paper Table 4); the exact count and SHA-256 are pinned in configs/exp.yaml at download time.\", \"RepoBench files are under MIT; CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf is under Meta's Llama community license; both permit research use as intended.\", \"CodeLlama-7b-Python-hf context window = 16,384 tokens per Code Llama release notes (Roziere et al., 2023).\", \"GPT-2 context window = 1024 tokens; gpt2 = 124M, gpt2-medium = 355M (Radford et al., 2019).\", \"RepoBench prompt markers are <cross_file>, <file>, </cross_file>, </file>, </MASK> per the released data_collector.py; cross_file snippets are concatenated in the order the bundle stores them.\", \"Edit Similarity normalization per paper \\u00a74.2 = strip leading/trailing whitespace, collapse internal whitespace runs, strip end-of-line Python comments before computing normalized Levenshtein in [0, 100]; EM = strict equality post-normalization; MAELS = paper's line-blended variant.\", \"Headline decoding = greedy, do_sample=False, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, max_new_tokens=64, stop on first newline or EOS, no repetition/length penalty; matches the paper's published completion protocol.\", \"GPT-2 truncation policy (60/40 head/tail up to MASK) is an explicit, documented choice; it deviates from the paper's internal handling (which is unpublished), and the truncation rate is recorded as a covariate.\", \"The RepoBench evaluator.py is preferred; if unavailable, metrics are replicated from \\u00a74.2 and cross-checked against paper Table 4 rows.\", \"Bootstrap CI uses 10,000 instance-level resamples with rng seed 42; McNemar uses continuity correction; alpha = 0.05 one-sided.\", \"Design targets a single GPU (1 \\u00d7 A100/H100 or 1 \\u00d7 RTX 4090 with int4 for the 7 B model); this sandbox cannot install packages, cannot reach the Hub, and per task.json's design-only note no execution is attempted.\", \"gpt2-medium is recorded as a one-line ablation note only, not as a re-run arm, per resolved choice #2.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Exact instance count and SHA-256 of the cross_file_first test slice are pinned at download time; confirmed ~1,000 from paper Table 4.\", \"If RepoBench's released evaluator.py cannot be located in the bundle, the metric reimplementation must be cross-checked against paper Table 4 before reporting; minor numerical drift is possible.\", \"GPT-2 truncation policy (60/40) is a documented choice but is not byte-for-byte identical to the paper's internal handling; sensitivity of ES to this split is not in scope of the headline run.\", \"The expected-direction priors are grounded in scale + domain specialization but cannot be quantified without a running experiment; specifically, the fraction of the ES gap attributable to truncation vs. scale vs. domain pretraining is left as a *follow-up*, not a deliverable.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-4a54cb59-832a-4fc0-ba31-9845ca935a49", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 23}
